    <string name="onboarding_welcome_title">Welcome to Birthday Sync</string>
    <string name="onboarding_welcome_text">The app automatically copies the birthdays from your contacts into a calendar of your choice and reminds you on the day itself.\n\nTo do this reliably in the background, it needs a few permissions. We’ll go through them step by step and explain what each one is for.\n\nEverything stays on your device – the app has no internet access.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_contacts_title">Access to your contacts</string>
    <string name="onboarding_contacts_text">The birthdays are stored in your contacts. For the app to find them, it needs read access.\n\nOnly names, birthdays and the assignment to accounts and groups are read, also in the background during the regular sync. The data stays on your device: the app doesn’t change any contacts, has no internet access and shares nothing.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_calendar_title">Access to your calendar</string>
    <string name="onboarding_calendar_text">For every birthday, the app creates an event in a calendar of your choice and keeps it up to date, for example when a birth date changes.\n\nFor this it needs to read and write calendars. Only its own birthday events are ever changed.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_notifications_title">Notifications</string>
    <string name="onboarding_notifications_text">If you like, the app lets you know at a fixed time when someone has a birthday today.\n\nWithout this permission, the notification stays silent.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_exact_alarm_title">On-time reminder</string>
    <string name="onboarding_exact_alarm_text">For the daily notification to arrive exactly at the set time and not sometime later, the app needs the permission for alarms and reminders.\n\nOn the next screen, turn on the switch for Birthday Sync and then come back here with “Back”.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_battery_title">Work in the background</string>
    <string name="onboarding_battery_text">Android likes to put background apps to sleep to save battery. Then the regular sync can fail.\n\nThe sync takes only a few seconds a day; the extra battery use is barely measurable. In the following list, choose “All apps”, then Birthday Sync, and select “Don’t optimize”.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_background_title">Remove background restriction</string>
    <string name="onboarding_background_text">Background activity is restricted for this app, so the sync and the notification don’t happen.\n\nIn App info, open “Battery” and choose “Unrestricted”. Then come back here with “Back”.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_hibernation_title">Don’t pause automatically</string>
    <string name="onboarding_hibernation_text">If you don’t open the app for a few months, Android pauses it and removes its permissions. The sync would then stop without you noticing – even though the app is meant to work in the background.\n\nOn the next screen, turn off “Pause app activity if unused” and then come back here with “Back”.</string>
